  [1mIncomplete is not approval.[0m Both gates were attempted and neither produced a review, so this design has been seen only
  by me and by you. That's recorded in the spec's process note rather than left implicit.

  ---

  Spec written to [38;5;153mdocs/hyperpowers/specs/2026-09-30-login-analytics-tracking-design.md[39m (not committed). Please review it
  and let me know if you want any changes before we move to the implementation plan.

  Three things in it are worth your attention specifically, since they're where I'd most expect you to disagree:

  1. [1m[38;5;153mlogin()[39m's signature doesn't change.[0m The literal request was a [38;5;153muserId[39m parameter; the spec deliberately doesn't add
     one, because nothing can supply a value. If you need the parameter to exist for a reason outside this repo — an
     interface someone else is coding against, say — tell me, because that changes the design.
  2. [1m[38;5;153mindex.html[39m stops working over [38;5;153mfile://[39m.[0m A real cost of the ES-module approach. Easy to reverse now by switching to
     approach B, much less so after other forms import the tracker.
  3. [1mNothing is ever sent anywhere.[0m This builds the queue and the [38;5;153mflush[39m seam; records accumulate in [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m until a
     backend exists. If you expected data to start flowing to an analytics service as part of this change, it won't,
     and that's a larger piece of work.

